+    "description": "Learn how to create clear, compelling openings for Instagram Reels and YouTube Shorts. Viral Hook Formula introduces eight practical approaches: curiosity gaps, pattern interrupts, bold statements, questions, stories, visuals, numbered lists and relatable POV scenes.\n\nAcross ten lessons, you will learn to write stronger first lines, plan effective opening visuals and connect every hook to a meaningful payoff. Practical examples and assignments help you choose the right approach for your audience, communicate your topic quickly and deliver the value your opening promises. You will also explore how to combine hook styles, use ChatGPT to develop relevant options and compare different openings to improve your content.\n\nDesigned for creators, small business owners and students, the course concludes with a practical project: creating eight original reels, one for each hook type, using a repeatable process for planning, reviewing and refining your ideas.",
+    "videos": [
+      {
+        "title": "HOOK FOUNDATIONS",
+        "description": "Understand what a hook does and how your first line, opening visual and promised payoff work together. Explore the eight hook types covered in the course, learn to recognise weak or misleading openings, and review your recent reels. You will begin building concise, relevant introductions that match the content you actually deliver.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 01  /  HOOK FOUNDATIONS\nThe First Three Seconds: Understanding\nHooks\nGive viewers a clear reason to pay attention from the opening moment.\nWhat a hook actually does\nA hook is the opening line and visual that introduce a reason to keep watching a reel or short\nvideo. It may raise a question, show an interesting result, introduce a problem or begin an\nunexpected action. The viewer should quickly understand what makes this particular video\nrelevant, useful or entertaining.\nThe course focuses on the first three seconds as a practical creative target. This is not a fixed\nplatform rule or a guarantee that a video will be recommended. The purpose is to remove a slow\nopening and communicate the idea promptly, while keeping speech natural and understandable.\nConnect attention with a useful promise\nA hook can help someone notice your content, but attention alone is not the finished job. The rest\nof the video must answer the question or deliver the result introduced at the start. This delivery is\nthe payoff. A clear promise also helps you decide which information belongs in the reel.\nIn the introductory demonstration, the presenter asks the viewer to stop scrolling and then\nexplains the technique she has just used. Treat that as an example to analyse, not a phrase to\nrepeat in every video. Your own opening should give a reason connected to your audience and\ntopic.\nKnow the eight approaches you will practise\nLater lessons combine these approaches, use ChatGPT to draft alternatives and compare two\nopenings for the same topic. There is no single format that fits every audience or video.\n- Curiosity gap opens a specific question; pattern interrupt introduces a relevant unexpected\naction, sound or visual.\n- Bold statement challenges familiar advice with a reason; question directly addresses an\naudience problem.\n- Story begins at an interesting moment; visual uses the opening image itself to communicate\ninterest.\n- List promises a clear number of points; POV recreates a specific situation the audience\nrecognises.\nUse the course's central rule\nA hook is a promise\nPair a specific opening line with a relevant first image, then deliver what they promise. Strong\nwording cannot compensate for a missing answer, an unrelated video or a claim the content\ncannot support.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1-2, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-3, 5-6.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 01  /  BUILD AND REVIEW YOUR OPENING\nThe First Three Seconds: Understanding Hooks\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nTwo illustrative English examples\nThese examples demonstrate the structure rather than guaranteeing performance. Notice how\neach includes something to say, something to show and a result that the video can actually\nprovide.\n- Product demonstration: \"Here is the print this stamp makes.\" Opening visual: hold the stamp\nabove a clean sheet of paper, ready to press. Payoff: show the stamping action and a clear\nclose-up of the actual print. The opening and result refer to the same product.\n- Study content: \"Turn this paragraph into three revision questions.\" Opening visual: show one\nshort paragraph with the number three on screen. Payoff: demonstrate three questions drawn\nfrom that paragraph. Keep all three questions visible long enough to read.\nWrite the opening before building the full reel\n1. Choose the viewer and topic. Name the audience and one specific idea. A narrow topic\nmakes the opening easier to understand than a broad promise to explain everything.\n2. Define the payoff. Write one sentence describing what the viewer will receive. Use this to\ncheck that your proposed hook is accurate before filming.\n3. Draft the line and first visual. Keep the spoken opening short and place the relevant\nsubject on screen immediately. Add a readable text overlay carrying the main idea.\n4. Rehearse and review. Read the line naturally, then watch the opening with sound muted.\nThe image and text should still make the topic clear.\nCorrect common opening problems\nIf the video begins with a blank screen, long greeting or logo animation, move the useful content\nearlier. If the line feels vague, name the exact problem or result. If it takes too long to say,\nremove extra words rather than rushing your voice.\nDo not diagnose every low-performing reel as a hook failure. The topic, content, delivery and\naudience fit also matter. Use the opening review to identify something concrete you can\nimprove, without promising views or virality.\nYour assignment\nWrite down the first line and describe the first visual of your last five reels. For each, identify the\npromise and where the video fulfils it. Rewrite one weak opening. Check its clarity, natural\ndelivery, readability without sound and connection to the actual payoff.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1-2, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-3, 5-6.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
