+        "description": "Learn to create curiosity by introducing a specific question, missing detail or surprising part of a process. Develop openings that give viewers a reason to continue, then plan a clear and timely payoff. You will practise writing five curiosity hooks while avoiding vague secrets, exaggerated claims and unnecessary delays.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 02  /  CREATE A QUESTION WORTH ANSWERING\nThe Curiosity Gap Hook\nOpen a meaningful question and reward the viewer with a clear answer.\nUnderstand the curiosity gap\nA curiosity gap is the space between what the viewer already sees or understands and one\ndetail they want to discover. The hook establishes the topic but leaves a specific question open.\nThe reel then closes that gap by showing or explaining the answer.\nThis approach suits demonstrations, useful tips and behind-the-scenes content when the missing\ndetail genuinely matters. The viewer needs enough context to care. Saying only \"you will not\nbelieve this\" with no visible subject creates uncertainty, but it does not explain why the answer\nis worth watching.\nUse a clear, honest formula\nThe course demonstrates phrases about an undisclosed secret. Keep the useful structure while\navoiding unsupported claims such as \"nobody knows this\" or \"every creator hides this.\" Describe\nthe specific detail you can show instead of inventing exclusivity.\nCuriosity formula\nStart with a recognisable topic or result, identify one unanswered detail, and reveal it through\nthe video. Example structure: \"One step changes this result.\" Show what the step is and how\nit fits the process.\nPlan the answer before recording\n1. Choose a useful missing detail. Identify an ingredient, process step, design choice or\nexplanation that belongs to your topic. The answer should be more substantial than a generic\nreminder to keep watching.\n2. Write the opening promise. State the question or withheld detail in one short line. Avoid\nadding a second mystery that the same video cannot resolve.\n3. Choose the opening image. Show the result, object or situation the question refers to. The\nvisual should make the gap easier to understand without revealing everything at once.\n4. Map the reveal. Decide when to show the answer and what evidence or demonstration\nmakes it clear. Then build the remaining script around that delivery.\nMake the waiting worthwhile\nThe source lesson places its answer near the end, but that is one structure rather than a rule.\nReveal the useful detail as soon as the story or demonstration supports it. Give relevant\ninformation along the way; repeated promises to reveal a secret create delay without value.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1-2, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-3, 5-6.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 02  /  EXAMPLES, CORRECTIONS AND PRACTICE\nThe Curiosity Gap Hook\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nThree illustrative English examples\n- Food preparation: \"What gives this sandwich its crunch?\" Opening visual: show the finished\nsandwich being cut, with filling partly visible. Payoff: reveal and demonstrate the crisp\nvegetable layer used in this sandwich. Avoid claiming it is a secret used by every restaurant.\n- Product packaging: \"There is one more detail inside this parcel.\" Opening visual: show an open\nbox containing the product, with a partly covered insert. Payoff: reveal the care-instruction\ncard and explain its purpose. Use this only when the detail is included.\n- Content creation: \"One choice comes before I film this reel.\" Opening visual: show a blank shot\nlist beside a written opening line. Payoff: explain the course practice of drafting the hook first,\nthen planning the reel to deliver its promise.\nRecognise a weak or misleading gap\nAn opening can be intriguing but still disappoint if the answer does not match the question. A\npromise about a specific technique should end with that technique, not an unrelated promotion.\nCheck the payoff in plain language: does it resolve exactly what you asked?\nAvoid presenting an ordinary tip as a universal secret or a guaranteed solution. For example, a\nbroad claim that one mistake explains all poor results is harder to support than a demonstration\nof one issue in a particular example. Make the claim no larger than the evidence in your reel.\nTighten the delivery\nRead the hook aloud and remove phrases that repeat the same promise. Replace \"stay until the\nvery end and I will finally tell you\" with a shorter setup, then move into the relevant explanation.\nIf the answer fits near the beginning, reveal it there and use the remaining time to show how it\nworks.\nKeep the hook visible in a short text overlay when useful. Do not hide all context just to preserve\nsuspense: a viewer should still recognise the topic and understand what question remains\nunanswered.\nYour assignment and review\nWrite five curiosity hooks for your own niche. Beside each, describe the opening visual, the\nexact answer and the point in the reel where it appears. Choose one and rehearse a short\nversion. Confirm that the question is specific, the reveal is meaningful and no unnecessary delay\nseparates the viewer from the promised value.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1-2, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-3, 5-6.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
