+        "description": "Explore how an unexpected action, sound or visual change can create an engaging opening. Learn to connect the interruption directly to your topic, pair it with a clear message and deliver the promised content. You will plan and rehearse an opening that feels purposeful, readable and practical to film.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 03  /  MAKE THE INTERRUPTION MEAN SOMETHING\nThe Pattern Interrupt Hook\nUse a relevant surprise to introduce the subject of your reel.\nWhat a pattern interrupt is\nA pattern interrupt changes an expected opening. Instead of beginning with a greeting or a\nstatic introduction, the reel starts with a noticeable action, sound, movement or change in\nframing. The difference can help draw attention, especially when similar videos use similar\nintroductions.\nThe effect is a creative device, not a reliable way to control every viewer or guarantee\ndistribution. Its purpose is to make the subject noticeable and lead immediately into relevant\ncontent. An interruption that has no connection to the topic may attract a glance without giving\nanyone a reason to continue.\nUnderstand the demonstration\nIn the course opening, the presenter tears a piece of paper and identifies it as an old content\nplan before introducing a new one. The action acts as a simple visual metaphor: an existing\napproach is being replaced. The accompanying line explains why the viewer has just seen it.\nYou can adapt that idea without using the same action. For example, turn over a rough draft to\nreveal a revised plan. The useful element is the relationship between the physical change and\nthe lesson that follows, not the dramatic gesture by itself.\nBuild the hook with three linked parts\n- Action: reveal, turn, lift, place or open something that already belongs to the topic.\n- Sound: use a recognisable sound from the real activity, such as an egg cracking into a bowl.\n- Framing: begin unusually close to a meaningful detail, then reveal the wider context.\nPattern interrupt formula\nUnexpected but relevant opening action + a short line that explains its meaning + an\nimmediate transition into the promised content. The movement should introduce the idea\nrather than become a separate performance.\nChoose one clear interruption\nStart with one deliberate change. Stacking a loud effect, sudden zoom, thrown object and\nseveral captions can make the idea harder to read. Plan an ordinary, controlled action that fits\nyour filming space. Keep the subject in focus and leave enough space for the movement. One\nclear action is easier to connect with the following shot than several simultaneous effects.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1-2, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-3, 5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 03  /  PLAN, FILM AND REVIEW\nThe Pattern Interrupt Hook\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nThree illustrative English examples\n- Content planning: \"This plan forgot the editing.\" Opening visual: turn over a crowded draft to\nreveal three clear production slots. Payoff: explain how the revised plan separates writing,\nfilming and editing. Use the contrast to introduce the planning method.\n- Cooking: \"Start with this egg.\" Opening visual and sound: crack an egg into a bowl in a clear\nclose-up. Payoff: continue directly into the preparation shown in the reel. The sound comes\nfrom the subject instead of an unrelated audio effect.\n- Fashion: \"Same scarf, a different finish.\" Opening visual: lift a scarf across the frame, then\nlower it to reveal its starting position. Payoff: demonstrate one complete styling change. Keep\nthe movement controlled and ensure the garment is easy to see.\nCreate a simple filming plan\n1. Name the content promise. Write what the viewer will learn or see. Choose an interruption\nthat represents this idea rather than adding an unrelated stunt.\n2. Rehearse the opening movement. Decide where the object begins, what moves and what\nthe camera reveals. Keep the action understandable within the opening moment.\n3. Connect it with one line. Say or display a short explanation of the action. If sound supplies\nthe surprise, also make the subject clear in the image or text.\n4. Continue without a reset. Move directly into the demonstration or explanation. Avoid a\nsecond introduction after the interruption, because it delays the promised content.\nCorrect the most common mistakes\nIf the action looks striking but the topic remains unclear, change the accompanying line or\nchoose an object that explains the subject better. If the sound is the only meaningful element,\nadd a clear image or text cue for viewers watching without audio.\nIf the opening feels crowded, remove extra effects and keep one movement. If viewers must\nwait through a long greeting after the action, shorten that transition. The interruption and payoff\nshould belong to the same idea.\nYour assignment and review\nPlan one unexpected opening for your next reel. Write its action, line, first visual and payoff,\nthen record a short rehearsal. Review it with and without sound. Check that the surprise is\nrelevant, the subject is understandable and the video can continue naturally into the promised\ncontent.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1-2, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-3, 5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
