+        "description": "Learn to create bold opening statements that challenge familiar advice without misleading your audience. This lesson explains how to choose a relevant belief, define the situation in which your alternative makes sense, and support the hook with a clear reason. You will practise turning strong opinions into useful, credible reel openings.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 04  /  UNDERSTAND THE FORMULA\nBold Statement (Contrarian) Hook\nChallenge familiar advice with a clear, honest explanation.\nWhat makes a statement contrarian?\nA contrarian hook questions something your audience commonly hears or does. Instead of\nrepeating familiar advice, it introduces a different view and gives people a reason to ask, 'Why\nwould that be better here?' The useful part is the explanation that follows the statement.\nThe course opens this lesson with 'Stop posting daily,' followed by a preference for three good\nreels over seven weak ones. Treat that comparison as an illustrative opinion about quality and\nworkload. It is not evidence that a particular posting frequency always produces better results.\nUse a claim, reason, and demonstration\nFor example, 'Stop posting just to fill the calendar' can lead into advice for a solo creator who is\nrushing unfinished ideas. Explain that, in this situation, reducing the workload creates room to\nplan clearer scripts. Do not turn that observation into a claim that daily posting is wrong for\nevery creator.\nPractical formula\nQuestion a familiar habit + specify when your advice applies + give a clear reason + show a\nuseful alternative. The opening can be bold; the explanation must remain accurate.\nBuild the hook step by step\n1. Choose advice your audience recognises. List common habits in your niche, such as\nposting every day, highlighting entire pages, or opening product videos with lengthy packaging\nshots. Pick one you can discuss specifically.\n2. Find a genuine limitation. Ask when the habit becomes less useful. Describe the situation\nand the practical tradeoff. If you cannot explain the limitation honestly, choose another idea\ninstead of forcing disagreement.\n3. Plan the payoff before filming. Decide what the viewer will see: a clearer script, a more\nselective set of notes, or the product working. Your opening should promise only what this\ndemonstration can deliver.\nKeep the opening easy to understand\nSay one short statement and move promptly into its reason. Use the first few seconds as a\ncreative guide, not a guarantee of distribution. A concise text overlay and a relevant first frame\nhelp communicate the claim when sound is off.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1, 3, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-3, 5-6.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 04  /  EXAMPLES AND PRACTICE\nBold Statement (Contrarian) Hook\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nThree illustrative English examples\nThese are adaptable teaching examples. Use the claims only when the accompanying\nexplanation fits the content you actually make.\n- Creator: 'Stop posting just to fill the calendar.' Opening visual: a crowded weekly plan beside\nan unfinished script. Scope and reason: if daily deadlines leave your ideas unclear, reduce the\nworkload. Payoff: show one rushed outline being revised into a focused script and explain your\nscheduling choice.\n- Student: 'Stop highlighting every line.' Opening visual: a page where nearly every sentence is\ncoloured. Scope and reason: if everything is marked, the markings no longer identify what you\nselected as important. Payoff: show a more selective page and explain why each marked\nsentence supports the topic.\n- Product: 'Stop opening this stamp demo with the box.' Opening visual: a finished stamp\nimpression beside its packaging. Scope and reason: in a demonstration about the printed\nresult, a long unboxing delays that result. Payoff: show the stamp being used and reveal the\nfinished design clearly.\nAvoid empty controversy\nA dramatic statement without a reason creates an argument rather than a useful lesson. Give\nthe reason immediately, then support it with a visible example. Avoid words such as 'always,'\n'never,' or 'everyone' unless the claim genuinely supports that scope.\nDo not invent results, statistics, or personal experience to make the claim sound authoritative.\nDescribe an opinion as an opinion and a demonstration as a demonstration. Disagree with the\nadvice respectfully; insulting people who follow it adds no practical value.\nCheck the promise against the finished reel\n- Can the viewer identify the familiar advice you are questioning?\n- Have you explained the situation in which your alternative applies?\n- Does the footage actually show the reason or alternative you promised?\n- Would the opening still be accurate if someone watched only that first line?\nYour assignment\nChoose one common piece of advice in your niche. Write a bold opening, a one-sentence\nexplanation, and a description of the first visual. Then outline the demonstration that will deliver\nthe payoff. Read the opening aloud, remove unnecessary words, and soften any claim that goes\nbeyond what your example can support.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1, 3, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-3, 5-6.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
