+        "title": "FIND THE RIGHT QUESTION",
+        "description": "Turn everyday audience problems into focused question hooks for Reels and Shorts. Learn to replace vague greetings with relevant questions, match the first visual to the problem, and provide a useful answer in the reel. Practical examples help you write openings that feel specific, natural and appropriate for your audience.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 05  /  FIND THE RIGHT QUESTION\nQuestion Hook\nOpen with a specific question your audience already cares about.\nStart with a recognisable problem\nA question hook invites the viewer to think about a situation that matters to them. Its strength\ncomes from relevance: a creator, student, or customer should recognise the problem and\nunderstand what the reel will help them explore. A question mark alone does not make an\nopening useful.\nThe course contrasts specific questions with a general greeting such as 'Hello, how are you?' A\ngreeting can be friendly, but it gives little information about the topic. Begin with the audience's\nproblem and add any necessary introduction after the opening.\nUse a specific situation and a clear question\nInstead of 'Do you struggle with content?', ask 'Can viewers understand your opening with the\nsound off?' The second version names something observable and gives the reel a clear task:\ndemonstrate how the opening communicates visually. Avoid putting several unrelated problems\ninto the same question.\nPractical formula\nAudience situation + precise problem or decision + a question the reel can answer. Pair the\nwords with a first frame that makes the situation visible.\nDevelop the hook in three steps\n1. Write down the audience's actual situation. Use questions people have asked, problems\nyou have observed, or a clearly labelled teaching example. Be specific about the task: naming\nassignment files, choosing a product size, or explaining a reel without audio.\n2. Ask about something you can show. Choose one decision, difficulty, or point of confusion.\nUse everyday language. A useful question should allow a concrete demonstration, comparison,\nor explanation rather than a vague motivational answer.\n3. Plan the answer and opening image together. Decide what appears in the first frame\nand what the viewer will learn by the end. If the question concerns product size, show the\nproduct and a relevant size reference rather than unrelated footage.\nAvoid unsupported diagnoses\nA weak opening can be worth reviewing, but do not claim to know why every viewer skips a reel.\nMany factors may affect a result. Ask an observable question instead of declaring an unverified\ncause. Keep the first line brief; the course's three-second target is a creative guideline, not an\nalgorithm rule.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1, 3, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 05  /  EXAMPLES AND PRACTICE\nQuestion Hook\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nThree illustrative English examples\nEach example names one concern, makes it visible immediately, and delivers an answer within\nthe reel. Adapt the details to your own audience instead of copying the wording into an\nunrelated topic.\n- Creator: 'Does your opening make sense on mute?' Opening visual: play the first moment of a\nsample reel without sound. Payoff: identify what is unclear, add a concise overlay or more\nrelevant opening shot, and compare the two versions. Keep the spoken question and text\noverlay focused on the same issue.\n- Student: 'Which file is your final assignment?' Opening visual: several similarly named files on a\ndemonstration screen. Payoff: show a simple naming example that includes the subject, task,\nand version, then identify the submitted copy.\n- Product: 'Will this stamp fit on your gift tag?' Opening visual: hold the actual stamp beside a\nsmall tag. Payoff: show the measured print area and a test impression on that tag. Place the\nreal print measurements beside the impression so viewers can compare them with the tag.\nMake the question easy to follow\nRead the line aloud in a natural voice. Remove the greeting, long setup, and repeated words. Put\nthe essential question on screen in a readable overlay, and check that the image supports it\nwhen sound is off.\nThe question should create a useful reason to continue. Begin answering promptly rather than\nrepeating 'wait until the end.' If the answer needs more explanation than a short reel allows,\nnarrow the question to one manageable part of the topic.\nCommon mistakes and corrections\n- Too broad: replace 'Want better results?' with a question about one task or observable\ndifficulty.\n- Too many questions: choose the one the reel answers most clearly and save the others for\nseparate videos.\n- False assumption: avoid telling every viewer they have a problem. Ask whether the specific\nsituation applies to them.\n- Missing payoff: show the answer, method, or comparison promised in the opening instead of\nswitching topics.\nYour assignment\nList three problems or decisions relevant to your audience. Turn each into one short question\nhook. For every question, write the opening visual and the answer you can demonstrate. Select\nthe clearest option, record a rough opening, and review it both with sound and on mute before\nplanning the rest of the reel.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1, 3, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
