+        "title": "CHOOSE THE TURNING POINT",
+        "description": "Learn to open a reel at the most interesting moment of a story. This lesson shows how to combine a clear time or place with an unexpected development, introduce background without slowing the opening, and deliver a meaningful resolution. You will practise shaping a real experience into a concise, engaging story hook.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 06  /  CHOOSE THE TURNING POINT\nStory Hook: Start in the Middle\nBegin at the turning point, then add only the background viewers need.\nWhat does starting in the middle mean?\nA story hook begins where something changes: a plan fails, a discovery appears, or a decision\nbecomes necessary. The viewer meets the situation at an interesting moment instead of hearing\na long history first. You can explain the essential background after giving them a reason to care\nabout the outcome.\nIn the course demonstration, a late-night message becomes the opening event for a story about\nchanging a content style. The useful structure is the specific moment followed by an unexpected\ndevelopment. A story does not need a dramatic crisis; a small, relevant surprise can be enough.\nUse time or place, then the twist\nA time cue such as 'Before class' or a place cue such as 'At my kitchen counter' helps the viewer\nenter the scene quickly. The twist should be specific: a file would not open, an ingredient was\nmissing, or a product test revealed a mistake. Avoid vague endings such as 'and everything\nchanged' unless you explain what changed.\nPractical formula\nA short time or place cue + the moment something changes + a clear reason to wonder what\nhappened next. Then add essential context, show the response, and resolve the story.\nBuild the opening from the event\n1. Choose a genuine, useful moment. Pick an experience you can describe accurately and\nconnect to your audience. If you are making a fictional demonstration or reenactment, identify it\nclearly rather than presenting an invented event as personal testimony.\n2. Find the first visible change. Identify the exact action or discovery that makes the story\ninteresting. Start there. Remove the long introduction, routine preparation, and details that do\nnot affect the viewer's understanding.\n3. Plan the resolution before filming. Write what you did next, what happened, and what\nthe viewer can take from it. The payoff must answer the question raised by the opening, even\nwhen the outcome is modest or imperfect.\nKeep the opening concise\nUse the first few seconds to establish the moment, not the entire plot. Read the line aloud and\nremove unnecessary words. The three-second target helps focus the writing; it does not\nguarantee attention, reach, or a particular result.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1, 3-4, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-2, 4-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 06  /  EXAMPLES AND PRACTICE\nStory Hook: Start in the Middle\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nThree illustrative English examples\nThese fictional scenarios illustrate the formula. Adapt them to a real experience, or label the\nvideo as a demonstration. Do not present invented events as something that happened to you.\n- Student: 'Before class, my presentation would not open.' Opening visual: a demonstration slide\nfile failing to load. Payoff: explain that the slides are needed immediately, open a saved PDF\nbackup and show the first slide. Finish by showing the backup folder the student will keep\nready next time.\n- Cooking: 'At dinner, I noticed the bread was missing.' Opening visual: sandwich filling beside an\nempty bread basket. Payoff: use an available wrap instead, add the filling, roll it and reveal the\nfinished meal. The story follows one practical adjustment from a missing ingredient to a\ncompleted dish.\n- Product: 'At my desk, the first stamp print was incomplete.' Opening visual: an imperfect\nimpression beside the stamp. Payoff: show that one corner missed the ink pad, ink the full\ndesign and make a second impression. End with the complete print beside the first attempt so\nthe improvement is visible.\nAdd background only when it helps\nAfter the opening event, give the minimum context needed to understand its importance. For\nthe presentation story, the viewer may need to know that class was starting soon. They do not\nneed the full history of the assignment before seeing what happened.\nContinue with the character's response and the result. Match the visuals to those steps. A simple\nsequence of problem, response, and outcome keeps the story understandable while preserving\nthe interest created by the opening.\nCommon mistakes and corrections\n- Starting too early: cut routine background and begin at the moment the plan changes.\n- Using an unclear twist: name the specific event instead of relying on 'something unbelievable\nhappened.'\n- Adding unrelated details: keep only the context that explains the problem, response, or\noutcome.\n- Ending without resolution: show what happened next and address the exact question created\nby the opening.\nYour assignment\nChoose one real moment from your life or work. Write a time-or-place opening that starts at its\nturning point. Add one sentence of background, the action you took, and the outcome. Plan a\nrelevant first visual, read the sequence aloud, and remove any detail that delays the story\nwithout helping the viewer understand it.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1, 3-4, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-2, 4-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
