+        "title": "UNDERSTAND THE VISUAL PROMISE",
+        "description": "Learn to build visual openings using close-ups, movement and unusual camera angles. This lesson explains how to make the subject clear without relying on audio, connect the opening image to the reel’s promise and avoid delayed introductions. Practise planning first frames that lead naturally into useful, relevant content.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 07  /  UNDERSTAND THE VISUAL PROMISE\nVisual Hooks: Make the First Frame Count\nCreate an opening image that introduces your topic clearly, even without sound.\nWhat makes a hook visual?\nA visual hook uses the opening image to introduce something worth noticing. It may show an\nappealing detail, an action already in progress or a view the audience does not usually see. In\nthe lesson demonstration, the presenter brings a red apple towards the camera. The image\nintroduces the object before the spoken explanation adds meaning.\nThe goal is not simply to make an attractive frame. The viewer should be able to recognise a\nsubject and anticipate what the reel will show. A useful test is to watch the opening with the\nsound off: can you identify the main object or situation, and does it connect to the content that\nfollows?\nThe formula and its three approaches\n- Close-up: isolate a useful detail, such as steam rising from food or a product's texture. Keep\nenough context for the detail to make sense.\n- Movement: bring the subject into view or begin a meaningful action immediately. The\nmovement should reveal information instead of merely shaking the camera.\n- Unusual angle: show a familiar subject from a fresh viewpoint, such as above a work surface.\nChoose the angle because it makes the process clearer.\nVISUAL HOOK FORMULA\nOpen with a relevant close-up, clear movement or unusual angle; reveal the subject quickly;\nfollow with the demonstration or explanation the image suggests.\nVisual hook versus pattern interrupt\nA visual hook communicates through what is seen. A pattern interrupt breaks an expected flow\nwith an unexpected action, sound or change. The two can overlap, but they are not identical. A\nquiet food close-up can work as a visual opening without a sudden surprise. An unexpected\nsound can be a pattern interrupt without explaining the subject visually.\nChoose the approach that serves the topic. You do not need to add a dramatic zoom to every\nreel. A clear image of the result may provide a better introduction to a careful demonstration.\nUse the timing guide sensibly\nThe course treats the first roughly three seconds as a creative planning window, not a platform\nrule. Remove blank frames and long logo animations before the subject appears. Start with the\nrelevant image and add introductions only when they help the viewer.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 4, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 07  /  PLAN, SHOOT AND CHECK\nVisual Hooks: Make the First Frame Count\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nA practical planning sequence\n1. Define the content first. Write what the reel will actually demonstrate: a recipe step,\nproduct feature or styling idea. This is the payoff that the opening must support.\n2. Choose one visual focus. Select a detail or action that represents that payoff. Decide\nwhether a close-up, movement or unusual angle makes it easiest to understand.\n3. Frame and record the opening. Keep the subject visible and the background simple.\nRecord a clear beginning rather than adding a blank lead-in. Check focus and avoid motion that\nhides the important detail.\n4. Connect it to the next shot. Move directly into the promised explanation. Add a short text\noverlay if the image needs context, then review the sequence both with sound and muted.\nIllustrative English examples\n- Food: open on steam rising from a freshly served bowl. Overlay: 'The final step in this soup.'\nPayoff: show the actual finishing step and how it fits the recipe.\n- Product: bring a customised stamp towards the lens, then reveal its printed impression.\nOverlay: 'From this design to this print.' Payoff: demonstrate the same design and stamping\nprocess.\n- Fashion: begin with a close-up of a scarf knot, then widen to show the full look. Overlay: 'One\nscarf, a simple knot.' Payoff: demonstrate the exact knot without switching to a different\nresult.\nCommon mistakes and fixes\nIf the opening is an abstract blur, give the object more space or reveal it sooner. If movement\nfeels distracting, slow or simplify it. If the text covers the detail, shorten or reposition it. If the\nfirst image promises a result the reel never shows, change the opening or supply that missing\ndemonstration.\nA visually unusual opening does not guarantee attention or reach. Judge it first on relevance,\nlegibility and whether the rest of the reel fulfils its promise.\nPractice assignment\nPlan three opening frames for your next topic: one close-up, one movement and one unusual\nangle. Film the strongest option. Watch it muted and check that the subject is clear, no\nintroduction delays it, any overlay is readable and the next shot delivers a logical continuation.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 4, 6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
