+        "title": "BUILD A USEFUL LIST",
+        "description": "Use numbered openings to communicate what a viewer can expect from your reel. Learn to choose a focused benefit, organise distinct points and keep the list manageable. This lesson also explains how to build towards a strong final point while delivering useful information throughout, supported by examples and a practical planning exercise.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 08  /  BUILD A USEFUL LIST\nList Hooks: Turn a Number into a Clear\nPromise\nStructure a short list around a specific benefit and deliver every promised point.\nWhat a number tells the viewer\nA list hook names a number and a specific kind of value. Instead of introducing a broad topic\nsuch as studying or content creation, it promises a defined set of ideas, mistakes, steps or tools.\nThe number helps the viewer understand the structure before the explanation begins.\nThe benefit must be as clear as the count. 'Three tips' gives little context; 'Three checks before\nfilming a product demo' tells a specific audience what the reel contains. A number organises\nyour promise, but it does not make weak, repetitive or unsupported advice useful.\nThe list-hook formula\nThe course recommends practising with three to five points. Treat this as a manageable writing\nguide, not a platform limit. Choose the number after selecting useful content; do not inflate the\ncount by splitting one idea into several near-identical items.\nThe final point can provide a satisfying conclusion, but calling it 'the most important' requires an\nhonest reason. It might combine the earlier ideas or address the most consequential mistake in\nyour particular example. If all points are equally useful, introduce them plainly without inventing\na ranking.\nLIST HOOK FORMULA\nNumber + specific topic or benefit + a clear reason to follow the list. Deliver the promised\nnumber of distinct points, with a strong final point when the content supports that order.\nGive value throughout the reel\nEach item should add something new. Explain the point, show a quick example and move on.\nAvoid delaying all useful information until the ending or repeatedly asking the viewer to wait. A\nstrong final item should reward attention after several useful points have already been\ndelivered.\nA simple structure is opening promise, point one, point two, point three and a brief conclusion. A\nvisible number or short label can help the viewer follow the sequence. The visual should\ndemonstrate the item whenever possible instead of showing unrelated footage.\nKeep the opening concise\nSay the count and benefit early, and begin the first point promptly. The course's roughly\nthree-second opening target is a creative guide, not a rule imposed by a platform. If the\nsentence is crowded, remove exaggerated adjectives and unnecessary setup before cutting\nuseful context.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 4-6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 08  /  WRITE AND DELIVER THE PAYOFF\nList Hooks: Turn a Number into a Clear Promise\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nPlan the list step by step\n1. Choose one audience and task. Name who the reel helps and what they are trying to do. A\nnarrow situation gives you a clearer list than a broad subject alone.\n2. Select three to five distinct points. Write one sentence for each. Remove duplicates and\nanything you cannot explain or demonstrate honestly within the planned reel.\n3. Arrange the sequence. Put the points in a useful order, such as preparation, action and\nfinal check. Reserve a strong conclusion only when it naturally follows from the other items.\n4. Write the opening and visuals. State the number and benefit. Plan one relevant shot per\npoint and check that the completed reel delivers exactly what its opening promises.\nIllustrative English examples\n- Creator: 'Three checks before recording your voiceover.' Opening visual: a microphone beside\na short checklist. Payoff: show room noise, microphone position and a test recording; the final\ncheck brings the earlier choices together.\n- Student: 'Three things I prepare before a revision session.' Opening visual: a cluttered desk\nbecoming ready for work. Payoff: demonstrate the topic plan, necessary materials and a short\npractice task, with one visible example of each item.\n- Product business: 'Three shots that show this stamp clearly.' Opening visual: the product\nbeside its printed impression. Payoff: show the whole stamp, a close-up of the design and a\ncomplete stamping demonstration.\nCommon mistakes and fixes\nA long list can become rushed when every point needs explanation. Reduce the count or split\nthe topic into separate reels. If points overlap, combine them. If an item is only a vague slogan,\nadd a concrete demonstration or remove it.\nDo not promise five items and deliver four, or repeat 'wait for the last one' instead of teaching.\nMake the count match the finished edit. If the last item is not demonstrably stronger, remove\nthat claim and let the useful sequence carry the reel.\nPractice assignment and review\nPlan one list reel with a clear audience, numbered opening and three to five points. For each\npoint, write its explanation and matching visual. Confirm that every item is distinct, useful and\ndelivered, the opening works with a readable overlay, and the ending completes the promised\nlist without an unsupported claim.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 4-6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
