+        "title": "FIND THE RECOGNISABLE MOMENT",
+        "description": "Create relatable openings by turning specific audience experiences into short POV scenes. Learn to identify everyday situations, choose recognisable details and coordinate on-screen text with acting and visuals. The lesson covers common mistakes, practical examples and an exercise for developing relevant hooks without relying on vague captions or exaggerated promises.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 09  /  FIND THE RECOGNISABLE MOMENT\nPOV Hooks: Make a Familiar Situation\nSpecific\nUse a recognisable everyday moment to introduce a scene your audience understands.\nWhat POV means in this lesson\nPOV stands for point of view. In this course, a POV hook introduces a familiar situation with a\nshort 'POV:' caption and then acts out the moment. The viewer is invited to recognise an\nexperience, reaction or routine that feels relevant to their own life.\nThe format is more than placing those three letters over any video. The caption needs a specific\nsituation, and the visual needs to show it. A person looking surprised beneath the words 'POV:\nlife is hard' gives very little context. A visible task, meaningful detail and clear reaction make the\nscene easier to understand.\nThe POV-hook formula\nStart with an audience you understand: students revising, office workers finishing a task or\ncreators checking an edit. Choose an ordinary experience that has a clear emotional turn, such\nas expecting to finish and discovering one more job.\nSpecificity comes from relevant details, not a longer sentence. A time, task, object or\ninterruption can make the scene recognisable. Include only what helps the intended audience\nunderstand it. A moment familiar to your viewers can be effective without being universal.\nPOV HOOK FORMULA\nPOV: + a specific audience situation + a recognisable moment or reaction. Show the situation\nimmediately, then develop the joke, observation or useful response promised by the opening.\nAct the situation rather than labelling it\nPlan what the audience sees first: an open textbook, a nearly closed laptop or an editing\ntimeline. Then decide the smallest action that communicates the change, such as reopening the\nbook, pausing before leaving or spotting a misspelled caption.\nThe expression, prop and caption should describe the same event. Keep the action readable\nwith the sound off, and use speech only when it adds something. You can show an exaggerated\nreaction for a clearly acted skit, but avoid presenting an invented experience as a factual\npersonal case study.\nWhat the format can and cannot promise\nA relatable scene may give viewers a reason to keep watching, but it does not guarantee shares,\ncomments or views. Review the match between your audience and situation first. The course's\nopening timing guidance is a creative prompt to get to the moment quickly, not a fixed platform\nrequirement.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 5-6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 09  /  WRITE, ACT AND REFINE\nPOV Hooks: Make a Familiar Situation Specific\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nA repeatable planning method\n1. List real audience routines. Write five tasks or moments your viewers encounter. Use your\nown relevant experience or what the audience actually tells you instead of assuming everyone\nhas the same problems.\n2. Find the small tension. Identify what changes in each moment: a plan meets an\ninterruption, an expectation meets reality or a small mistake becomes visible. Keep it\nunderstandable without a long explanation.\n3. Write the POV line. Name the situation in one short sentence. Replace generic wording with\none detail that matters to that audience, then remove anything the picture already explains.\n4. Plan the scene and payoff. Choose the opening prop, action and reaction. Decide how the\nreel resolves the moment through a joke, observation or practical response. Film and review the\ncaption and action together.\nIllustrative English examples\n- Student skit: 'POV: you open tomorrow's revision checklist for the first time.' Opening visual: a\nlong checklist beside an untouched notebook. Payoff: the character reacts, then chooses one\nsection to begin; the scene delivers the stated situation.\n- Office skit: 'POV: your laptop is closing when the last request arrives.' Opening visual: a hand\nstopping the lid beside a clearly staged work message. Payoff: show the recognisable pause\nand decision, then reopen the laptop to complete the final task.\n- Creator skit: 'POV: you export the reel and then spot the typo.' Opening visual: a completed\nexport beside a visibly misspelled caption. Payoff: the creator catches the error and returns to\ncorrect that caption.\nCommon mistakes and fixes\nIf the scene feels like a generic meme, replace the broad caption with a specific routine and\nmeaningful prop. If viewers need several sentences of explanation, simplify the situation. If the\ncaption describes one event but the acting shows another, revise them together.\nAvoid stacking several unrelated frustrations into one short opening. Choose one moment and\nlet the reaction develop. Relevance matters more than making the situation louder or more\ndramatic.\nPractice assignment\nWrite five everyday moments for your audience and turn each into a POV hook. For every option,\nadd the first visible action and the intended payoff. Film one. Check that the caption is readable,\nthe scene makes sense muted and the ending develops the exact situation introduced.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 5-6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 2-5.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
