+        "title": "CHOOSE AND COMBINE YOUR APPROACH",
+        "description": "Bring the eight hook types together in a practical content workflow. Learn to combine complementary approaches, brief ChatGPT for relevant options and compare openings before filming. The lesson introduces practical testing and review, then concludes with a final project: write one hook of each type and produce eight reels that fulfil their promises.",
+        "notes": "SKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 10  /  CHOOSE AND COMBINE YOUR APPROACH\nYour Hook Formula: Combine, Draft and\nTest\nChoose suitable hook types, use ChatGPT thoughtfully and improve through practical observation.\nThe principle that connects every hook\nA hook is a promise made by the first line and visual. Choose it from your audience, topic and\nintended payoff. No single type fits every reel; the eight approaches below give you different ways\nto introduce that promise.\nHook typeCore approach\nCuriosity gapOpen a specific question and deliver its answer.\nPattern interruptIntroduce a relevant unexpected action, sound or change.\nBold statementChallenge familiar advice with an honest explanation.\nQuestionAsk about a specific audience problem.\nStoryBegin at an interesting moment with brief context.\nVisualLead with a meaningful close-up, movement or angle.\nListPromise a defined number of useful points.\nPOVAct a recognisable, specific everyday situation.\nCombine two types for one clear purpose\nCombine approaches that support the same promise. A visual can show the problem a question\nnames, or a story can introduce an unanswered question. Keep the opening focused on one idea.\n- Question + visual: 'Why is this stamp print patchy?' Opening visual: a clear imperfect impression.\nPayoff: demonstrate the actual cause and correction in your example.\n- Story + curiosity: 'I was ready to export when I noticed this caption.' Opening visual: the editing\npreview. Payoff: reveal the real error and show how you corrected it; use the personal claim only\nif it happened.\nBrief ChatGPT with useful constraints\nGive ChatGPT the audience, topic, hook type and promised payoff. Use its suggestions as drafts;\nchoose natural wording and check that the claim matches your footage.\nCOMPLETE ENGLISH DRAFTING PROMPT\nMy niche: [niche]. My audience: [specific viewers]. Reel topic: [topic]. Hook type: [type or\ntwo-type combination]. What my reel can honestly deliver: [payoff]. Write 10 distinct hook\noptions in natural English, each 1-2 lines. For each, include one sentence describing the opening\nframe and the specific promise the reel must fulfil. Avoid invented facts, exaggerated results\nand claims of guaranteed engagement. Aim for an opening line that takes roughly three\nseconds to say naturally.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1, 5-6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-3, 5-6.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES1 / 2\n\nSKILLOMATEVIRAL HOOK FORMULA\nLESSON 10  /  COMPARE, OBSERVE AND COMPLETE THE PROJECT\nYour Hook Formula: Combine, Draft and Test\nDetailed lesson notes | Practice, checks and application\nCompare two drafts before filming\nSelect two hooks for the same topic and payoff. Score each from 1-10 for clarity, attention\npotential and whether its promise can be delivered. Explain each score using the wording and\nplanned visual. These scores are editorial judgements, not measured performance or predictions\nof views.\nUse this prompt: 'Compare hook A: [A] and hook B: [B] for [audience], with this payoff: [payoff].\nScore clarity, attention potential and deliverability from 1-10. Explain the trade-offs, identify\nunsupported promises and improve the clearer option without adding claims.'\nRun a practical comparison carefully\n1. Change the opening. Prepare two versions with different hooks and the same main\ncontent, payoff and overall structure. Keep other controllable choices similar so the opening is\nthe main planned difference.\n2. Record the conditions. Note what changed and how each version was shared. Similar\npublishing conditions are useful, but separate posts may reach different people; this is practical\nobservation, not a fully controlled experiment.\n3. Review appropriate measures. Where available, compare early retention or\nviewer-continuation rates at the same point, using the same metric definition. Record the\nsample size and inspect whether viewers stay for the payoff. Raw view totals alone cannot\nisolate the hook's effect.\n4. Repeat and refine. Look for patterns across several comparable topics. A single pair does\nnot prove one formula always wins. Use observations to improve the next opening while\nchecking the quality of the full reel.\nFinal project: eight hooks, eight reels\nWrite one hook for each of the eight types in your niche, then shoot eight reels that deliver those\npromises. For each, save the hook, opening-frame plan, intended payoff and finished reel.\nReview your choices and identify one improvement to try next.\nFinal publishing check\n- The opening is concise, specific and free of a delayed logo or blank introduction.\n- The first visual and readable overlay support understanding without sound.\n- Every statement is honest, and the reel delivers the promised answer or demonstration.\nSources: Course Notes pp. 1, 5-6; Chatbot Knowledge Base pp. 1-3, 5-6.\nENGLISH EDITION  |  STUDENT LESSON NOTES2 / 2",
